WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
<v DANA>One person, holding a used needle with the sharps container just down the hall, told

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.680
<v DANA>themselves... "just this once."

3
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.800
<v DANA>We'll be exploring the consequences of that decision today.

4
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.320
<v DANA>I'm Dana.

5
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.560
<v RAY>I'm Ray. Glad you're with us.

6
00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:24.903
<v DANA>Ray, you worked patrol for years before you moved into training new officers.

7
00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:28.690
<v DANA>When someone brand new shows up, what's the first thing you notice?

8
00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.957
<v RAY>How BADLY they want to get it right.

9
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:34.850
<v RAY>And I can usually tell early on which little habits are going to stick with them.

10
00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.890
<v DANA>Which is today's subject, really: the habits people build around needles and other

11
00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.410
<v DANA>sharp objects at work.

12
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:47.640
<v DANA>More people now inject their own medicine at work, for things like diabetes and

13
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.000
<v DANA>allergies. Does that really reach a police station?

14
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.640
<v RAY>It does. Accidental needle sticks are especially common in healthcare, but the risk

15
00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.440
<v RAY>is real and growing at other jobs too.

16
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.920
<v RAY>And a sharp is any medical needle or other sharp object that can puncture or cut

17
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:03.680
<v RAY>skin.

18
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.080
<v DANA>So take a new dispatcher, someone who answers calls and sends officers out.

19
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.280
<v DANA>It's a busy afternoon and the phones keep ringing.

20
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.680
<v DANA>Her insulin syringe sits on her desk beside her headset, and the sharps container is

21
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.600
<v DANA>in the break room down the hall.

22
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.520
<v DANA>Walk me through that one needle.

23
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.600
<v RAY>It starts with a plan for handling it and getting rid of it, BEFORE the cap comes

24
00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.240
<v RAY>off. She'd use a needle alternative if one's possible, and switch on any safety

25
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.480
<v RAY>feature once she's done.

26
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.120
<v DANA>Once she's done, though, the syringe is empty.

27
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.960
<v DANA>Isn't most of the risk over?

28
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.027
<v RAY>Not at all. A little blood stays inside.

29
00:01:39.053 --> 00:01:42.840
<v RAY>And because it's so sharp, there's a high chance it accidentally pierces someone

30
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.560
<v RAY>else's skin and exposes them to that blood.

31
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:50.240
<v DANA>Not a comfortable thought. So now it's used and in her hand.

32
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.240
<v DANA>Where does it go?

33
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.160
<v RAY>Straight into the sharps container.

34
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.467
<v RAY>Hospitals and clinics spell out more: a used needle isn't bent, broken, recapped,

35
00:01:58.653 --> 00:02:02.760
<v RAY>removed or separated from the syringe, and it goes in immediately or as soon as

36
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.360
<v RAY>possible after use.

37
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.467
<v DANA>With the phones still going, that hallway's going to feel long.

38
00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:10.680
<v DANA>What do you say to someone who'd rather wait?

39
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.880
<v RAY>Waiting is what the plan is for.

40
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.840
<v RAY>You decide how it gets to the container before you start, use a needle alternative

41
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.920
<v RAY>if there is one, switch on the safety feature if it has one, and it goes in the

42
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.840
<v RAY>container the MOMENT you're done.

43
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:28.030
<v DANA>That walk down the hall sounds simple when nobody's busy.

44
00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:32.510
<v RAY>It does. What happens when someone skips it is worth hearing out.

45
00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.260
<v DANA>This one's about a single needle and the trash can it landed in.

46
00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.820
<v DANA>Chris works records on nights, keeping the reports and files in order, and has a

47
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.740
<v DANA>story from one of those nights.

48
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.020
<v DANA>Go ahead, Chris.

49
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.500
<v CHRIS>It was near the end of my shift.

50
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.380
<v CHRIS>I'd just taken my insulin at my desk.

51
00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.660
<v CHRIS>The sharps container was down the hall, past the copier.

52
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:59.100
<v CHRIS>I knew better from training. But I had reports to finish.

53
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:04.020
<v CHRIS>So I thought, just this once, and dropped the needle in the trash can under my

54
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:05.620
<v CHRIS>desk.

55
00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:10.620
<v CHRIS>At shift change, when the morning crew came in, Jacob came around to empty the cans.

56
00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.660
<v CHRIS>He grabbed my bag by the top and lifted it out.

57
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:17.860
<v CHRIS>Then he stopped, pulled his hand back, and looked at it.

58
00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.300
<v CHRIS>I keep thinking about how close that container was.

59
00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.700
<v DANA>That's hard to hear. What's really at stake when a used needle goes into a trash

60
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:28.740
<v DANA>bag?

61
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.780
<v RAY>Whatever blood is left inside it.

62
00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.300
<v RAY>A needle stick can expose someone to bloodborne pathogens, meaning germs carried in

63
00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.900
<v RAY>blood, like H-I-V, hepatitis B and hepatitis C.

64
00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.500
<v RAY>Those could lead to severe health problems.

65
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.300
<v DANA>And Jacob was only emptying a can.

66
00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.900
<v DANA>How does that sit with you?

67
00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:51.700
<v RAY>Badly. A sharp that's thrown away wrong can hurt someone down the line who NEVER

68
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:52.700
<v RAY>touched it.

69
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.460
<v DANA>Chris was only trying to get reports done.

70
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.820
<v DANA>What do you make of that?

71
00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:02.220
<v RAY>One shortcut can cause a serious injury, maybe an infection that changes a life.

72
00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:05.700
<v RAY>Where you put a needle directly affects someone else's risk.

73
00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.660
<v DANA>Just a regular bag at the end of a regular night.

74
00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:15.450
<v DANA>That one stays with you.

75
00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:20.730
<v RAY>It does. So let's look at what actually stands between a used needle and somebody's

76
00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:21.450
<v RAY>hand.

77
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.120
<v DANA>Picture someone at the station about to empty the trash bins.

78
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.467
<v DANA>They pull on the thickest gloves in the supply closet and figure they're covered.

79
00:04:31.493 --> 00:04:32.640
<v DANA>Do you think they are?

80
00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.331
<v RAY>Not against a needle. EVEN thick gloves can be pierced by one.

81
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.320
<v DANA>So the thickest pair in the closet still won't stop a needle.

82
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.160
<v DANA>What are the gloves good for, then?

83
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.360
<v RAY>Plenty. Heavy-duty gloves made of nitrile, latex, vinyl or neoprene help protect

84
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.040
<v RAY>hands from cuts and hazardous substances.

85
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.960
<v RAY>They're P-P-E, personal protective equipment, meaning gear you wear.

86
00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:59.813
<v RAY>But P-P-E alone is less effective against needle sticks than engineering controls.

87
00:04:59.867 --> 00:05:03.440
<v RAY>Those are things like a sharps container, which deal with the needle itself instead

88
00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:04.640
<v RAY>of your hands.

89
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.240
<v DANA>Now say an agency is deciding how to protect everyone who empties trash.

90
00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.520
<v DANA>It can buy cut-resistant gloves for all of them, or add sharps containers where

91
00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.880
<v DANA>people actually use needles. Gloves for everybody sounds quicker.

92
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.131
<v DANA>Which way would you go?

93
00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:23.600
<v RAY>The containers. A proper sharps container is the most effective protection, because

94
00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.693
<v RAY>it takes the hazard away before anyone can touch it.

95
00:05:26.747 --> 00:05:31.200
<v RAY>It closes the needle inside. That's been stated for manufacturing settings, so I'll

96
00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.520
<v RAY>keep it there: safety-engineered containers like that reduce needle sticks and the

97
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.880
<v RAY>spread of bloodborne pathogens, the germs carried in blood.

98
00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.440
<v DANA>But a container only works if people actually walk the needle over to it.

99
00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.000
<v DANA>Why don't they always?

100
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.160
<v RAY>Because it's the easy path, and people lean that way.

101
00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.680
<v RAY>I trained a lot of classes, and somebody was usually hunting for the shorter route.

102
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.320
<v RAY>Engineering controls and training are what have to beat that habit when it comes to

103
00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.648
<v RAY>getting rid of sharps.

104
00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.880
<v DANA>I'd have pulled on the thickest pair and gone straight for that trash bag.

105
00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.480
<v DANA>Now I'd trust those gloves a lot less.

106
00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:10.470
<v DANA>Picture yourself pulling on a pair of thick gloves before a trash run.

107
00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:14.230
<v RAY>Then ask where the nearest sharps container is, and whether it's close enough that

108
00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.550
<v RAY>people actually walk a needle over to it.
